(4.1 Order type was too simple) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The system still treated every approved setup as a limit order.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(That is too simple for the playbook.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In practical trading terms:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- A support/resistance rejection or retest often waits for price to pull back into the entry) Tj
T*
(area, so a limit order can make sense.) Tj
T*
(- A direct breakout often waits for price to continue through a level, so a stop pending order) Tj
T*
(may be the correct MT5 order type.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The bot must not force every strategy into the same broker order shape.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4.2 Broker rejections need clearer explanation) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Two order attempts were rejected with Invalid price.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This likely happened because the order request did not match the current market quote and) Tj
T*
(pending order type rules. The report layer should explain this in trading language, not only as) Tj
T*
(a raw MT5 retcode.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4.3 Accepted pending orders did not fill) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Four pending orders were accepted by MT5 but later cancelled because price did not trigger them) Tj
T*
(during the activation window.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(That is not automatically bad. The playbook says stale pending entries should be cancelled. But) Tj
T*
(we need clearer evidence showing:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Which setup created the order.) Tj
T*
(- Which order type was used.) Tj
T*
(- Where bid/ask was at the time.) Tj
T*
(- Whether the order was a pullback entry or continuation entry.) Tj
T*
(- Why it was cancelled.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4.4 Clean range remains the biggest strategy filter) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
